WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:02.413
<v Examiner>Define poisoning.

2
00:00:02.413 --> 00:00:05.834
<v Examiner>What's the difference between acute and chronic poisoning?

3
00:00:05.834 --> 00:00:09.197
<v Examiner>And when should someone call the National Poisons Centre?

4
00:00:09.647 --> 00:00:12.455
The hook: acute is all at once, chronic builds up.

5
00:00:12.455 --> 00:00:14.084
And oh eight hundred, poison.

6
00:00:14.534 --> 00:00:15.159
Poisoning.

7
00:00:15.159 --> 00:00:20.097
Harm from a substance that's swallowed or touched: chemicals like heavy metals,

8
00:00:20.097 --> 00:00:22.223
pesticides and household cleaners.

9
00:00:22.223 --> 00:00:23.723
It's usually accidental.

10
00:00:23.723 --> 00:00:28.723
And her slide says most things can be poisonous, depending on how much is taken,

11
00:00:28.723 --> 00:00:32.349
the person's age and size, whether they're already unwell,

12
00:00:32.349 --> 00:00:35.099
and the other medicines it might react with.

13
00:00:35.549 --> 00:00:39.403
Acute poisoning: the poison enters the body in a short time,

14
00:00:39.403 --> 00:00:42.229
like a toddler swallowing household cleaner.

15
00:00:42.229 --> 00:00:47.688
Chronic poisoning: it builds up gradually in the body, like heavy metals over months.

16
00:00:48.138 --> 00:00:49.529
Poisonous or venomous.

17
00:00:49.529 --> 00:00:52.818
Poisonous: you touch it or eat it, and you get hurt.

18
00:00:52.818 --> 00:00:55.158
A poisonous plant, berry or mushroom.

19
00:00:55.158 --> 00:00:58.384
Venomous: it bites or stings you, and you get hurt.

20
00:00:58.384 --> 00:01:03.380
New Zealand has very few venomous creatures: bees and wasps, the katipō spider,

21
00:01:03.380 --> 00:01:07.807
the white-tailed spider, the bluebottle jellyfish, and some stingrays.

22
00:01:09.257 --> 00:01:11.056
The National Poisons Centre.

23
00:01:11.056 --> 00:01:14.138
Oh eight hundred, seven six four, seven six six.

24
00:01:14.138 --> 00:01:16.514
That spells oh eight hundred, poison.

25
00:01:16.514 --> 00:01:18.955
It's free, and open twenty-four hours.

26
00:01:18.955 --> 00:01:24.157
People should call it when someone has swallowed a medicine, a household product,

27
00:01:24.157 --> 00:01:27.496
a chemical, or a poisonous plant, berry or mushroom.

28
00:01:27.496 --> 00:01:31.093
When someone has taken too much medication, an overdose.

29
00:01:31.093 --> 00:01:33.854
When someone has breathed in harmful fumes.

30
00:01:33.854 --> 00:01:37.322
When a toxic substance splashes onto the eyes or skin.

31
00:01:37.322 --> 00:01:42.203
And for advice about symptoms from a recreational drug, like vomiting, pain,

32
00:01:42.203 --> 00:01:44.001
or an abnormal heart rhythm.

33
00:01:44.001 --> 00:01:48.882
But if the person is unconscious or has stopped breathing, call one one one.

34
00:01:49.332 --> 00:01:51.042
Where the marks go missing.

35
00:01:51.042 --> 00:01:53.892
Her formative mini exam three, question four:

36
00:01:53.892 --> 00:01:57.882
the nurse gave Trevor half a dose of his antibiotic by mistake.

37
00:01:57.882 --> 00:01:59.782
Which step is not appropriate?

38
00:01:59.782 --> 00:02:01.809
Calling oh eight hundred poison.

39
00:02:01.809 --> 00:02:05.039
Half a dose is a medication error, not a poisoning.

40
00:02:05.039 --> 00:02:09.663
You document it, complete an incident report, and contact the prescriber.

41
00:02:10.113 --> 00:02:10.673
<v Examiner>Once more.

42
00:02:10.673 --> 00:02:12.523
<v Examiner>Poisoning and the Poisons Centre.

43
00:02:14.973 --> 00:02:19.800
Poisoning: harm from a substance swallowed or touched, usually accidental,

44
00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:23.974
and almost anything can poison, depending on dose, age and size.

45
00:02:23.974 --> 00:02:28.735
Acute is all at once, like a child swallowing cleaner; chronic builds up,

46
00:02:28.735 --> 00:02:29.909
like heavy metals.

47
00:02:29.909 --> 00:02:34.148
Poisonous, you eat or touch it; venomous, it bites or stings you.

48
00:02:34.148 --> 00:02:39.300
The National Poisons Centre: oh eight hundred, poison, free, twenty-four hours,

49
00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:44.648
for swallowed products, overdoses, fumes, splashes and recreational drug symptoms.

50
00:02:44.648 --> 00:02:47.387
Unconscious or not breathing: one one one.
